{
	"nodes": [
		{
			"id": "header",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## Psychology\n\nDomain hub: perception, attention, learning, memory, emotion, motivation, language, social cognition, development and therapy models.\n\n[[Psychology Map|Open the domain map]] - snapshot of 2026-09-27; the map's *In this folder* section is the live view.",
			"x": 0,
			"y": 0,
			"width": 2260,
			"height": 170,
			"color": "#4CC38A"
		},
		{
			"id": "t0_0",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Perception]]**\n*process* - 5 sources\n\nSensation and perception distinguished, psychophysical thresholds, and the constructive character of perceptual experience.",
			"x": 30,
			"y": 360,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 178,
			"color": "#4CC38A"
		},
		{
			"id": "t0_1",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Attention]]**\n*process* - 8 sources\n\nSelection, capacity limits, sustained attention and the evidence for separable attention systems.",
			"x": 30,
			"y": 598,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 178,
			"color": "#4CC38A"
		},
		{
			"id": "t0_2",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Attention and executive function]]**\n*framework* - 7 sources\n\nAttention as selection and executive function as a family of separable control processes, with the measurement gap between tasks and everyday life.",
			"x": 30,
			"y": 836,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 202,
			"color": "#4CC38A"
		},
		{
			"id": "t0_3",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Working memory]]**\n*process* - 7 sources\n\nHolding and manipulating information over seconds: capacity limits, executive control and measurement issues.",
			"x": 30,
			"y": 1098,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 178,
			"color": "#4CC38A"
		},
		{
			"id": "g0",
			"type": "group",
			"label": "Perception and attention",
			"x": 0,
			"y": 310,
			"width": 420,
			"height": 1006
		},
		{
			"id": "t1_0",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Learning and conditioning]]**\n*process* - 5 sources\n\nClassical, operant and observational learning as three routes to behaviour change, and how cognitive accounts absorbed conditioning paradigms.",
			"x": 490,
			"y": 360,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 202,
			"color": "#4CC38A"
		},
		{
			"id": "t1_1",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Memory processes]]**\n*process* - 4 sources\n\nEncoding, storage and retrieval across memory systems, the reconstructive nature of recall, and which practice manipulations actually work.",
			"x": 490,
			"y": 622,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 202,
			"color": "#4CC38A"
		},
		{
			"id": "t1_2",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Episodic memory]]**\n*process* - 4 sources\n\nMemory for personally experienced events: encoding conditions, retrieval cues, and the reconstruction problem.",
			"x": 490,
			"y": 884,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 178,
			"color": "#4CC38A"
		},
		{
			"id": "t1_3",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Procedural memory and habit]]**\n*process* - 7 sources\n\nSkill learning, habit formation and the shift from goal-directed to automatic control.",
			"x": 490,
			"y": 1122,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 178,
			"color": "#4CC38A"
		},
		{
			"id": "t1_4",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Decision-making]]**\n*process* - 6 sources\n\nJudgment under uncertainty: heuristics, biases, framing effects and their interpretation.",
			"x": 490,
			"y": 1360,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 178,
			"color": "#4CC38A"
		},
		{
			"id": "t1_5",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Motivation and reward]]**\n*process* - 6 sources\n\nDrive, expectancy and value accounts of why behaviour persists, and how reward is measured.",
			"x": 490,
			"y": 1598,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 178,
			"color": "#4CC38A"
		},
		{
			"id": "g1",
			"type": "group",
			"label": "Learning and memory",
			"x": 460,
			"y": 310,
			"width": 420,
			"height": 1506
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_0",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Emotion regulation]]**\n*framework* - 10 sources\n\nEmotion components and theories, common regulation strategies, and why self-report, physiology and behaviour diverge.",
			"x": 950,
			"y": 360,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 178,
			"color": "#4CC38A"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_1",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Social cognition]]**\n*framework* - 8 sources\n\nHow people perceive, interpret and reason about other minds, and the interpretive frameworks used to explain differences.",
			"x": 950,
			"y": 598,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 178,
			"color": "#4CC38A"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_2",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Language]]**\n*process* - 4 sources\n\nLanguage structure, development and the classical localisation models with their modern qualifications.",
			"x": 950,
			"y": 836,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 178,
			"color": "#4CC38A"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_3",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Personality models]]**\n*framework* - 7 sources\n\nTrait models, dimensional measurement and the debate about consistency across situations.",
			"x": 950,
			"y": 1074,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 178,
			"color": "#4CC38A"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_4",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Alexithymia]]**\n*framework* - 10 sources\n\nDifficulty identifying and describing one's own feelings: its facets, why its main questionnaire is contested, its relation to interoception, and the evidence in autism, trauma and ADHD.",
			"x": 950,
			"y": 1312,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 226,
			"color": "#4CC38A"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_5",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Rumination and repetitive negative thinking]]**\n*process* - 5 sources\n\nRepetitive negative thinking as a transdiagnostic process: its forms, its links with PTSD symptoms and with both poles of bipolar disorder, a candidate brain substrate, and what CBT trials show about reducing it.",
			"x": 950,
			"y": 1598,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 274,
			"color": "#4CC38A"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_6",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Self-concept and identity]]**\n*framework* - 10 sources\n\nNegative self-concept and identity disturbance as clinical constructs: how complex PTSD and borderline personality disorder describe them, the memory and identity theory, whether treatment changes self-concept, and the evidence in ADHD and autism.",
			"x": 950,
			"y": 1932,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 274,
			"color": "#4CC38A"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_7",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Shame and guilt]]**\n*framework* - 5 sources\n\nTwo self-conscious emotions that research separates: how shame and guilt differ, their links with depression and PTSD, their role after trauma in autistic adults and in help-seeking, and why most of the evidence is correlational.",
			"x": 950,
			"y": 2266,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 250,
			"color": "#4CC38A"
		},
		{
			"id": "g2",
			"type": "group",
			"label": "Emotion and social",
			"x": 920,
			"y": 310,
			"width": 420,
			"height": 2246
		},
		{
			"id": "t3_0",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Developmental perspectives and attachment]]**\n*framework* - 11 sources\n\nLifespan developmental theories, attachment concepts, and how early relationships are studied without over-reading them.",
			"x": 1410,
			"y": 360,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 202,
			"color": "#4CC38A"
		},
		{
			"id": "t3_1",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Therapy models overview]]**\n*framework* - 3 sources\n\nThe main psychotherapy orientations, what each targets, and how their evidence is generated and compared.",
			"x": 1410,
			"y": 622,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 178,
			"color": "#4CC38A"
		},
		{
			"id": "t3_2",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[CBT and its evidence base]]**\n*framework* - 7 sources\n\nThe CBT model, its behavioural roots, how its effects are tested, and the design constraints that come with psychological treatments.",
			"x": 1410,
			"y": 860,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 202,
			"color": "#4CC38A"
		},
		{
			"id": "t3_3",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Dialectical behaviour therapy]]**\n*framework* - 5 sources\n\nA skills-based cognitive-behavioural therapy for intense emotion and self-harm: what it targets, what it teaches, how it has been adapted, and how strong the trial evidence is in borderline personality disorder, complex PTSD presentations and bipolar disorder.",
			"x": 1410,
			"y": 1122,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 274,
			"color": "#4CC38A"
		},
		{
			"id": "t3_4",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[EMDR]]**\n*framework* - 4 sources\n\nEye movement desensitisation and reprocessing: a trauma-focused therapy that pairs recall of distressing memories with eye movements, what its eye-movement component adds, and how strong its evidence is in adult PTSD, youth PTSD and trauma-exposed bipolar disorder.",
			"x": 1410,
			"y": 1456,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 274,
			"color": "#4CC38A"
		},
		{
			"id": "t3_5",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Acceptance and commitment therapy]]**\n*framework* - 3 sources\n\nA third-wave cognitive behavioural therapy built on the psychological flexibility model: its six named components, the laboratory evidence for them, and how strong the trial evidence is for trauma-related symptoms, autism and ADHD.",
			"x": 1410,
			"y": 1790,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 250,
			"color": "#4CC38A"
		},
		{
			"id": "t3_6",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Mindfulness-based interventions]]**\n*framework* - 6 sources\n\nStructured programmes that train mindfulness, from established protocols to mindfulness-informed therapies: what they target, what their components show in the laboratory, and how strong the evidence is in PTSD, bipolar disorder, ADHD and autism.",
			"x": 1410,
			"y": 2100,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 250,
			"color": "#4CC38A"
		},
		{
			"id": "g3",
			"type": "group",
			"label": "Development and therapy",
			"x": 1380,
			"y": 310,
			"width": 420,
			"height": 2080
		},
